  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe forecast area sits in between large surface high pressure over \nsouth central Canada and surface low pressure over the Southern\nHigh Plains. This is advecting moister air into the Northern \nPlains. Through this afternoon and tonight, high pressure will \ngradually churn eastward towards Ontario, while surface low \npressure deepens and propagates northeastward with associated \nshortwave aloft lifting through the area. This will result in \nprecipitation currently organized over South Dakota to lift into\nthe state. \n\nCurrently, most hi-res models suggest that the heaviest \nprecipitation will remain along and south of I94 east of the \nMissouri River, and then a little further north as one moves further \neast across the state. However, historically speaking, models tend \nto have a hard time resolving large Canadian high pressure systems \nin that they tend to saturate things too far north. And often\ntimes, saturation through the column is difficult under the\nnorthern edge of the observed precipitation shield. In other \nwords, even if organized radar echoes make it to I94 and north, \nthey may have a difficult time squeezing out meaningful \nmoisture.\n\nIn regard to precipitation types, surface temperatures will likely \nremain warmer than the NBM due to cloud cover. However, even \nthe NBM90 maintains most temperatures near or below freezing. \nThis would make sense as BUFKIT model forecast soundings suggest\nthat, assuming dry air aloft is able to be overcome, deep \nsaturation should allow for temperatures to wetbulb out below \nfreezing near the ND/SD border. This favors snow development, \nthough duration could be fairly limited. Overall, accumulating \nsnow remains possible mainly south of I94, and mainly on grassy \nsurfaces. NBM ensemble probabilities of measurable snow are in \nexcess of 70 percent for much of the area east river, south of \nI94, and west of Highway 281. However, the probability of an \ninch or more is now down to around 30 percent for parts of the \nsame geographic area. Of note, some soundings do show a loss of\nice on the backside as precipitation begins pulling off to the \neast. Depending on whether or not surface temperatures dip below\nfreezing, this could result in brief freezing rain or freezing \ndrizzle.\n\nAs any precipitation begins to move off to the east, very low-level \nstratus and possibly fog will develop in its wake. Depending on how \nstrong near-surface winds are, patchy dense fog could develop,\nespecially over elevated terrain in the southwest. Skies should\nbegin to clear out from west to east on Tuesday with flow aloft\nbecoming more zonal through the middle of the week, and \nsouthwesterly towards the end of the workweek. Overall, this \nshould result in a gradual warming trend with temperatures \nbecoming seasonably warm for the latter half of the week with \nhighs mostly in the 40s north to 50s south. \n\nTuesday afternoon could see light rain mainly in the northwest as \nweak shortwave energy lifts through the region, while Wednesday \ncould see rain in the southeast. Otherwise and overall, mostly dry \nconditions should prevail Tuesday through Thursday night. After \nwhich, periodic low precipitation chances, mostly in the form of \nrain, are in the current forecast to finish out the week and into \nearly next week.",
